adjudication.rationale:
In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the authorization check is:
```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```
This is a normal `!==` string comparison (not constant-time). Also, there is no guard that `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` must be non-empty. If `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is set to an empty string (as suggested by `.env.example` default `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""`), a request can satisfy the check by supplying an empty-valued header with the configured name; similarly for a single-space secret if configured that way. That means the endpoint can be unintentionally left effectively unauthenticated under common misconfiguration.

Yes. In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, after a successful `safeParse`, the code does:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

So whenever the refreshed token response lacks `refresh_token` (common for OAuth refresh flows), it injects the literal placeholder string. In `packages/app-store/office365calendar/lib/CalendarService.ts`, the parsed data is merged into the existing credentials:

```ts
o365AuthCredentials = { ...o365AuthCredentials, ...(tokenResponse.success && tokenResponse.data) };
await prisma.credential.update({ data: { key: o365AuthCredentials } });
```

Because `tokenResponse.data.refresh_token` is forced to the placeholder, it will overwrite the real stored refresh token and then be persisted via `prisma.credential.update`, breaking future refreshes. (GoogleCalendar passes existing credentials object with an existing refresh_token, so it may not trigger there, but Office365 uses the response JSON where refresh_token may be missing.)
